  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(t)=0$ or $f(t)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(t)>0,$ then $f(t)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(t)<0,$ then $f(t)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $f(t)$ has a relative minimum at $t=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{′}(a)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(t)$ changes its sign from negative to positive (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,5)$ | $5$ | $(5,7)$ | $7$ | $(7,8)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative minima at $t=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the temperature of the room reached a relative minimum at $t=5.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
